L109: ## Object Interfaces
L110: 
L111: Object interfaces allow you to create code which specifies which methods and properties a class must implement, without having to define how these methods or properties are implemented. Interfaces share a namespace with classes, traits, and enumerations, so they may not use the same name.
L112: 
L113: Interfaces are defined in the same way as a class, but with the `interface` keyword replacing the `class` keyword and without any of the methods having their contents defined.
L114: All methods declared in an interface must be public; this is the nature of an interface.
L115: 
L116: In practice, interfaces serve two complementary purposes:
L117:   * To allow developers to create objects of different classes that may be used interchangeably because they implement the same interface or interfaces. A common example is multiple database access services, multiple payment gateways, or different caching strategies. Different implementations may be swapped out without requiring any changes to the code that uses them.
L118:   * To allow a function or method to accept and operate on a parameter that conforms to an interface, while not caring what else the object may do or how it is implemented. These interfaces are often named like `Iterable`, `Cacheable`, `Renderable`, and so on to describe the significance of the behavior.
L119: Interfaces may define cite80†magic methods to require implementing classes to implement those methods.
L120: 
L121: > Note:
L122: >
L123: > Although they are supported, including cite81†constructors in interfaces is strongly discouraged. Doing so significantly reduces the flexibility of the object implementing the interface. Additionally, constructors are not enforced by inheritance rules, which can cause inconsistent and unexpected behavior.
L124: ### `implements`
L125: 
L126: To implement an interface, the `implements` operator is used. All methods in the interface must be implemented within a class; failure to do so will result in a fatal error. Classes may implement more than one interface if desired by separating each interface with a comma.
L127: 
L128: Warning
L129: A class that implements an interface may use a different name for its parameters than the interface. However, as of PHP 8.0 the language supports cite82†named arguments , which means callers may rely on the parameter name in the interface. For that reason, it is strongly recommended that developers use the same parameter names as the interface being implemented.
L130: 
L131: > Note:
L132: >
L133: > Interfaces can be extended like classes using the cite83†extends operator.
L134: > Note:
L135: >
L136: > The class implementing the interface must declare all methods in the interface with a cite84†compatible signature . A class can implement multiple interfaces which declare a method with the same name. In this case, the implementation must follow the cite84†signature compatibility rules for all the interfaces. So cite85†covariance and contravariance can be applied.
L137: ### Constants
L138: 
L139: It's possible for interfaces to have constants. Interface constants work exactly like cite86†class constants . Prior to PHP 8.1.0, they cannot be overridden by a class/interface that inherits them.
L140: ### Properties
L141: 
L142: As of PHP 8.4.0, interfaces may also declare properties. If they do, the declaration must specify if the property is to be readable, writeable, or both. The interface declaration applies only to public read and write access.
L143: A class may satisfy an interface property in multiple ways. It may define a public property. It may define a public cite87†virtual property that implements only the corresponding hook. Or a read property may be satisfied by a `readonly` property. However, an interface property that is settable may not be `readonly`.
L144: Example #1 Interface properties example
L145: 
L146:     `<?php
L147:     interface I
L148:     {
L149:         // An implementing class MUST have a publicly-readable property,
L150:         // but whether or not it's publicly settable is unrestricted.
L151:         public string $readable { get; }
L152: 
L153:         // An implementing class MUST have a publicly-writeable property,
L154:         // but whether or not it's publicly readable is unrestricted.
L155:         public string $writeable { set; }
L156:         // An implementing class MUST have a property that is both publicly
L157:         // readable and publicly writeable.
L158:         public string $both { get; set; }
L159:     }
L160: 
L161:     // This class implements all three properties as traditional, un-hooked
L162:     // properties. That's entirely valid.
L163:     class C1 implements I
L164:     {
L165:         public string $readable;
L166: 
L167:         public string $writeable;
L168: 
L169:         public string $both;
L170:     }
L171: 
L172:     // This class implements all three properties using just the hooks
L173:     // that are requested.  This is also entirely valid.
L174:     class C2 implements I
L175:     {
L176:         private string $written = '';
L177:         private string $all = '';
L178: 
L179:         // Uses only a get hook to create a virtual property.
L180:         // This satisfies the "public get" requirement.
L181:         // It is not writeable, but that is not required by the interface.
L182:         public string $readable { get => strtoupper($this->writeable); }
L183: 
L184:         // The interface only requires the property be settable,
L185:         // but also including get operations is entirely valid.
L186:         // This example creates a virtual property, which is fine.
L187:         public string $writeable {
L188:             get => $this->written;
L189:             set {
L190:                 $this->written = $value;
L191:             }
L192:         }
L193: 
L194:         // This property requires both read and write be possible,
L195:         // so we need to either implement both, or allow it to have
L196:         // the default behavior.
L197:         public string $both {
L198:             get => $this->all;
L199:             set {
L200:                 $this->all = strtoupper($value);
L201:             }
L202:         }
L203:     }
L204:     ?>`
L205: ### Examples
L206: Example #2 Interface example
L207: 
L208:     `<?php
L209: 
L210:     // Declare the interface 'Template'
L211:     interface Template
L212:     {
L213:         public function setVariable($name, $var);
L214:         public function getHtml($template);
L215:     }
L216: 
L217:     // Implement the interface
L218:     // This will work
L219:     class WorkingTemplate implements Template
L220:     {
L221:         private $vars = [];
L222: 
L223:         public function setVariable($name, $var)
L224:         {
L225:             $this->vars[$name] = $var;
L226:         }
L227:         public function getHtml($template)
L228:         {
L229:             foreach($this->vars as $name => $value) {
L230:                 $template = str_replace('{' . $name . '}', $value, $template);
L231:             }
L232: 
L233:             return $template;
L234:         }
L235:     }
L236: 
L237:     // This will not work
L238:     // Fatal error: Class BadTemplate contains 1 abstract methods
L239:     // and must therefore be declared abstract (Template::getHtml)
L240:     class BadTemplate implements Template
L241:     {
L242:         private $vars = [];
L243:         public function setVariable($name, $var)
L244:         {
L245:             $this->vars[$name] = $var;
L246:         }
L247:     }
L248:     ?>`
L249: Example #3 Extendable Interfaces
L250: 
